[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
Inter-VLAN routing on the Campus 1 multilayer switch (mswitch0) is healthy; no SVI fault is present. The user’s path from VLAN 10 to VLAN 30 transits mswitch0’s SVIs (172.16.10.254 and 172.16.30.254), which are both up and correctly addressed. Evidence: mswitch0 shows vlan10@br0 172.16.10.254/24 UP and vlan30@br0 172.16.30.254/24 UP, with connected routes for 172.16.10.0/24 and 172.16.30.0/24. From PC5 (172.16.10.5), default via 172.16.10.254 is set, and pings to both 172.16.10.254 and 172.16.30.254 succeed. Cross-VLAN reachability is normal: PC5 -> PC12 (172.16.30.12) returns 0% loss, and PC12 -> PC5 replies with TTL=63, indicating a single routed hop through mswitch0 as expected. A topology-wide probe further shows consistent 0% loss between VLAN 10 and VLAN 30 hosts.

Mechanism: If either SVI were down/misconfigured, default-gateway pings would fail and cross-VLAN ICMP would not traverse; instead, all tests succeed with normal latency and TTL behavior. Localization: mswitch0, interfaces vlan10 and vlan30 on bridge br0. Root cause: No fault found on mswitch0; the SVIs are operational and routing correctly (issue likely transient or client-specific).

Corrective fix: No network changes required. If the symptom recurs, validate the client’s IP/gateway settings and host firewall, and capture ARP/ICMP on mswitch0 to isolate a host-side or transient condition.
=== END ===